    "rich_text_YLUJGg": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_ppxitK": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What Are Diamonds?",
            "use_h1": false
          }
        },
        "text_Pbxqta": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A diamond is a naturally occurring gemstone composed of carbon atoms arranged in a specific crystalline structure. It is renowned for its exceptional hardness, brilliance, and rarity, making it one of the most sought-after gemstones in the world. Diamonds are formed deep within the Earth's mantle under high pressure and temperature conditions and are brought to the surface through volcanic eruptions. </p><p></p><p>The earliest recorded discovery of diamonds dates back to the 4th Century BC in India, marking the beginning of their storied history. Today, diamonds maintain their allure and prestige, standing as timeless symbols of beauty and luxury on Earth.</p>",
            "enlarge_text": true
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_ppxitK",
        "text_Pbxqta"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "height": "medium",
        "text_alignment": "left",
        "full_width": false,
        "color_scheme": "default"
      }
    },
